<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Panna State</h1><p class="st-meta">1675 CE – 1950 CE (approx.) · capital Panna</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Bundela kingdom of Chhatrasal in Bundelkhand, famous for its diamond mines and as a holy centre of the Pranami faith.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhatrasal">Chhatrasal</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bundelas">Bundela</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a>, rebelled against Mughal rule from the 1670s and built an independent kingdom with its capital at Panna. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> help under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baji-rao-i">Baji Rao I</a> he defeated a Mughal invasion in 1729; on his death his realm was divided among his heirs and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwa</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharajas</a> of Panna ruled the eastern share of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhatrasal">Chhatrasal's</a> kingdom. The state was known for its diamond mines, which gave it revenue for centuries. Chhatrasal's guru <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahamati-prannath">Prannath</a> made Panna a sacred centre of the Pranami sect. Under British paramountcy it was a salute state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bundelkhand-agency">Bundelkhand Agency</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Panna joined <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/vindhya-pradesh">Vindhya Pradesh</a> in 1948 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a> in 1956. The diamond mines at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majhgawan-diamond-mine">Majhgawan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panna-national-park">Panna National Park</a> lie in its former territory.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/panna-state.jpg" alt="Panna State" width="320" height="208"><figcaption>Image: Xufanc · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Panna_state.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1675 CE – 1950 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Panna</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Chhatrasal, Yadvendra Singh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bundeli</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Panna</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1812&amp;focus=panna-state" aria-label="Panna State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6810 1268 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Panna State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7130 1524L7130 1517M7130 1524L7364 1601M7130 1524L7364 1602M7130 1524L7160 1530"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7130 1517h0M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0M7160 1530h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7130 1524h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7130 1524h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Panna. <a href="/atlas?year=1812&amp;focus=panna-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
